#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e475613-ff86-5655-a966-121d622b0bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interior Angles of a Regular Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "789de0a7-45d5-544c-a969-e875ef116dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789de0a7-45d5-544c-a969-e875ef116dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507773} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c242367-088a-5aaa-85d0-7576ac6b6d63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e7a888c-cec5-5044-9f5d-6a42f018a041" :question/math-academy-id "q-134549" :question/problem "Determine the measure of angle $\\alpha .$\n\n$\\alpha =$  \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, angle $\\alpha $ is an interior angle of a regular octagon.\n\nA regular octagon is an $8$-sided polygon where all the interior angles have the same measure.\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles for the $n$-sided polygon equals\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\times (n-2).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a regular octagon is\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\times (8-2)=1\\,{080}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the measure of each interior angle is \n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{1\\,{080}^{∘}}{8}={135}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f6c0ca1-297a-5f25-bccf-c74d0c565c24" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Perimeter of a Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c32ff3-e119-59e1-9eed-671d1486d368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27c32ff3-e119-59e1-9eed-671d1486d368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507647} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f34133c-427e-53d9-8698-dcabc78b675d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "160e2a67-96cf-5111-8246-74329f1cd311" :question/math-academy-id "q-134550" :question/problem "Given that all four squares shown in the diagram are congruent, find the perimeter $p$ of square $B.$\n\n$p=$  \n{{field-1}}$cm$" :question/worked-solution "Since all the squares in the graph are congruent, their sides are of equal length. \n\nFrom the graph, the measure of one of the sides is $5\\,cm$, so all four sides of square $B$ have length $5\\,cm.$\n\nThe perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\np=4\\cdot 5=20\\,cm.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30fc8daf-a6eb-537b-b19b-06ad5504b5e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons Given Side and Angle Measures"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9e93a6-f563-5f14-80b7-9d9784360bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d9e93a6-f563-5f14-80b7-9d9784360bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507799} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0833e5-39f9-58f0-b668-4023e8567dac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34ed8780-12db-5c6f-9281-2e2a475215ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-134595" :question/problem "Polygon $A$ is congruent to polygon{{field-1}} (enter the polygon's label)." :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n* they have the same number of sides,\n\n* all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n* all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare polygons $B$ and $D$ to $A.$\n\n* The polygon $A$ is an octagon (it has $8$ sides). So, the polygons $B$ and $D$ can't be congruent to the polygon $A$ because they do not have $8$ sides.\n\n* On the other hand, $A$ and $C$ have the same shape: they have congruent corresponding sides, and since they are regular polygons, congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $A≅C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0730375d-ba61-5f0a-85f3-23c9535f7a38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable That Makes a Given Quadrilateral a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15afaf43-187f-5d31-999f-e1b1c9a4e9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15afaf43-187f-5d31-999f-e1b1c9a4e9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507625} :answer-field/id #uuid "759b510a-b989-59da-81ab-d1d02eec0dfc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9331587-ba62-51b2-9497-b1e12f18138c" :question/math-academy-id "q-134596" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ given that $\\beta =(2x+3)^{∘}.$\n\n$x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\beta $ is an angle obtained after bisecting one of the interior angles of a square by a diagonal. Therefore, we must have \n\n$$\n\\beta =\\frac{{90}^{∘}}{2}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\beta  & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ (2x+3)^{∘} & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+3 & =45 \\\\ 2x & =42 \\\\ x & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e475613-ff86-5655-a966-121d622b0bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interior Angles of a Regular Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a560515d-f0bc-56e0-9c3a-94762fd4cc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a560515d-f0bc-56e0-9c3a-94762fd4cc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507830} :answer-field/id #uuid "395ed277-0ca9-5298-b00a-892a1e31f04c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0199ac32-0250-5816-84be-6c71995ddb1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-134600" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\gamma $, the angle shown in trapezoid $D.$\n\n$\\gamma =$  \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's label the vertices of trapezoid $D$ as follows:\n\n![](images/14/14814c5373405af8211f12b18f591c7a9c34645bfc8396b8304d6fcc73f5a539.png)\n\n \n\nIn step 1, we found that measures of the interior angles of a regular octagon equal\n\n$$\nm∠O=m∠N={135}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles for the trapezoid equals ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\gamma $ by summing the measures of the interior angles of the trapezoid $D.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M+m∠N+m∠O+m∠P & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ \\gamma +{135}^{∘}+{135}^{∘}+\\gamma  & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {270}^{∘}+2\\gamma  & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\gamma  & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ \\gamma  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac53e171-0a02-5b74-8a3a-465dbecda9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Involve Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5109a18c-60b0-56ed-8fb4-7e25ce741d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5109a18c-60b0-56ed-8fb4-7e25ce741d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507719} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbcadfd-384e-5510-9480-433f1a05aef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf6b20ed-8b8a-511e-9502-26f8a79629b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-134601" :question/problem "Given that $\\delta =(3y-15)^{∘},$ find the value of $y.$\n\n$y=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the two bases of the trapezoid $D$ are parallel, angles $\\delta $ and $\\gamma $ in the graph are alternate interior angles and consequently, they have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta  & =\\gamma ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta  & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ (3y-15)^{∘} & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ 3y-15 & =45 \\\\ 3y & =60 \\\\ y & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5231e20f-0e57-517c-bae9-3a152234d5ab" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Exterior Angles of a Regular Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df22157-7c87-5d6c-8bb7-14b16e5f9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df22157-7c87-5d6c-8bb7-14b16e5f9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507629} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e665187-2a28-5f13-bb23-18072eab0afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e422e657-fc67-5f57-8e3f-8fd6f2f37149" :question/math-academy-id "q-134616" :question/problem "Find the measure of the exterior angle $\\omega .$\n\n$\\omega =$  \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\omega $ is the sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a regular octagon and a square.\n \n\n![](images/25/2586359b460d4e4ce0324b21caa9328f1d319ebdefa5f7118aaa4719973cb79d.png)\n\n \n\nFor any polygon, the exterior angles sum to ${360}^{∘}.$ In a regular octagon, there are $8$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{8}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a square, there are $4$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{4}={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, since the shaded angle is the sum of the measures of exterior angles of a regular octagon and a square, it must have a measure of\n\n$$\n{45}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}={135}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f6c0ca1-297a-5f25-bccf-c74d0c565c24" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Perimeter of a Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6229feb1-b95d-595e-a375-c675e4dcfbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6229feb1-b95d-595e-a375-c675e4dcfbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507741} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb4c37a-2397-501c-afb5-87d9ae5c11c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "922cc406-bf6e-549f-8f16-dbc5eef7f87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-134617" :question/problem "Find the perimeter of the entire figure.\n\n$p=$  \n{{field-1}}$cm$" :question/worked-solution "The perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides.\n\nSince there are $24$ sides in total, and sides of the given figure are equal with length $5\\,cm$, we have \n\n$$\np=24\\cdot 5=120\\,cm.\n$$"}]}
